# apn-autoconfig-modem quirk table v1
#
# Capabilities that are NOT portable across modems, keyed by what the modem
# reports about itself. Identity does not belong here and never will: the 3GPP
# core reads are uniform across vendors, and the one genuinely divergent read
# (ICCID) is handled by an ordered attempt list in the adapter rather than by a
# table lookup, because that works without knowing the vendor at all.
#
# Format, tab-separated:
#
#   <manufacturer>	<model>	<firmware>	<key>	<value>
#
# `manufacturer` and `model` match the reply to AT+CGMI and AT+CGMM exactly.
# `firmware` matches AT+CGMR and is `*` unless an entry genuinely depends on a
# firmware revision. `manufacturer` and `model` may not be `*`: a quirk that
# applies to every modem is not a quirk.
#
# **An absent entry means "not tested here", never "probably behaves like the
# others".** A modem gets no capability from this table until someone has
# watched it work on real hardware and recorded what they saw. That is the same
# rule the backend contract applies to capability reporting, for the same
# reason: guessing produces a control that fails at the worst moment instead of
# one that is honestly absent.
#
# Every entry must carry a comment naming the command that was run and the
# response that was observed, so a later reader can tell a measured entry from
# an inherited assumption.
#
# The table is deliberately empty. 0.14.0 needs no vendor-specific capability:
# everything it does — port resolution, identity, registration, signal, soft
# reset — is standard. The first entries are expected in 0.15.0, where the
# Fibocom connection path needs behaviour that genuinely differs by device.
